    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Benelli Showed the World Its New Tornado Naked Tre 903 Concept at EICMA 2026 and It's a Triple!\nOpel's New Leapmotor-Developed Compact SUV Expected to Start Production in September 2028\n20 photos\nPhoto: Opel\nBack in the day, Opel used to be GM's most popular European subsidiary. The Detroit-based automaker controlled the German automaker through 2017, when French colossus Groupe PSA acquired both Opel and Vauxhall from General Motors. Both marques became subsidiaries of Stellantis in 2021, further diluting their characters. Oh, and it's about to get weirder in 2028!\nPer a media release from Opel, its chief executive officer and the big kahuna at Leapmotor Global have signed a deal according to which the Chinese brand will help Opel develop a new compact SUV . Before going any further, Leapmotor is known as Zhejiang Leapmotor Technology Co., Ltd., and this entity controls 49 percent of the international division that runs all overseas businesses.\nStellantis is listed with 51 percent, and Leapmotor Global is the consumer-facing branding of Leapmotor International. Pretty straightforward thus far, right? As part of the Opel-Leapmotor agreement, said compact SUV will use components from Leapmotor, styling and chassis engineering from Opel, and so forth. Production will be handled by the Stellantis Zaragoza plant in Spain.\nThe cited media release reveals that sales are expected to start in 2028, yet a Zhejiang Leapmotor Technology filing with the Hong Kong Stock Exchange reveals that Leapmotor is committing 100 million euros under the cooperation agreement, which is a relatively small amount of money when you think about it.\nEngineering and development work is scheduled to kick off by the end of the year, continue into 2027, and part of 2028 to boot. The anticipated SOP date is September 2028, meaning that Opel's Chinese crossover would hit dealers as a 2029 model. In the first half of 2028, production readiness will be the focus.\nPhoto: Leapmotor The contractual arrangement further stipulates that Opel retains full responsibility for the overall product cost target, while Leapmotor has to make commercially reasonable efforts to support that objective. You can easily interpret this as making sure the newcomer remains commercially viable, but if you ask me, that's cost-cutting lingo right there.\nOpel will also conduct durability, static, and dynamic evaluations, and the evaluation results are to be discussed with Leapmotor. Regarding the Chinese partner, supporting Opel in achieving the crossover's overall total product cost target by supplying core components also sounds like a very cost-sensitive program.\nIf you have ever driven a Stellantis-era Opel or Vauxhall, you already know their material quality and reliability leave much to be desired. Instead of bashing the new collab, I'd wager that a Leapmotor-flavored Opel has the makings of a better overall package than a Stellantis-only product.\nMy biggest concern here is that Opel risks losing its role as a bona fide developer to become an integrator. The agreement paints a reasonable division of labor until you consider the finest of details. While there is nothing wrong with outsourcing parts, will Opel retain sufficient engineering authority to make the finished product recognizably Opel?\nThe collab's terms also indicate that Leapmotor has more to gain from a strategic standpoint, with said collaboration providing the Chinese party firsthand insight into European customers' preferences, more experience with European vehicle development requirements, and improvements in engineering know-how. And lest we forget, Stellantis is already building a Leapmotor model at the group's Zaragoza plant in Spain.\nSaid model is the B10 crossover, namely a compact that may very well become the half-brother of the yet-unnamed Opel crossover. Under this production arrangement, Leapmotor found a way to avoid China-origin vehicle duties while also lowering logistics costs. On that note, where would the new sport utility vehicle fit into Opel's current sport utility vehicle range?\nFollowing the canning of the Crossland, the German marque currently has the B-segment Mokka, the B-/C-segment Frontera, and the C-segment Grandland. Another compact sounds like an overlap, but as you already know, compact means many things nowadays. Due to Leapmotor's engineering, we can imagine a lower-cost model slotting between the Frontera and Grandland in the crossover range.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2029 Opel C-SUV 2028 Opel C-SUV Opel C-SUV Opel Leapmotor\nDownload: CONNECTED TRANSACTION NEW OPEL C-SUV COOPERATION AGREEMENT (PDF)\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Would You Pay New Prius Money for This Dodge Challenger SRT Hellcat?",
